xt": "C:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "imipramine"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(PO)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "100mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "nocte"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(medications"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "should"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "titrated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "according"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "symptoms"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "resolution)"}]}, {"id": "body-27404", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading2", "text": "Oppositional Deviant Disorder", "runs": [{"text": "Oppositional"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Deviant"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Disorder"}], "level": 2, "list": {"level": 1, "numberingId": "359"}}, {"id": "body-27405", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Oppositional defiant disorder (ODD) is listed in the DSM-5 under Disruptive, impulse-control, and conduct disorders and defined as \"a pattern of angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, or vindictiveness.", "runs": [{"text": "Oppositional"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "defiant"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "disorder"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(ODD)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "listed"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "DSM-5"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "under"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Disruptive,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "impulse-control,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "conduct"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "disorders"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "defined as \"a pattern of angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, or vindictiveness."}]}, {"id": "body-27406", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "This behavior is usually targeted toward peers, parents, teachers, and other authority figures, including law enforcement officials. Unlike Conduct Disorder (CD), those with ODD do not generally show patt